\id 2CO Original Douay-Rheims (1582/1610)
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h 2 Corinthians
\toc1 The Second Epistle of Paul to the Corinthians.
\toc2 2 Corinthians
\toc3 2CO
\mt1 The Second Epistle of Paul to the Corinthians.
\is THE ARGUMENT OF THE SECOND EPISTLE TO THE CORINTHIANS.
\ip FOR the time when this Epistle was written, look the Argument of the epistle to the Romans: to wit, about the eighteenth year after his conversion, & our Lord's passion, because in the 11. chapter he maketh mention of 14 years, not only after his Conversion, as to the Galatians , but \f - \ft \+it 2. Cor. 11. 2.\+it*\f* \f - \ft \+it Gal. 2. 1.\+it*\f* also after his rapt, which seemeth to have been when he was at Hierusalem Act. 9, 26. four years after his Conversion (Gal. 1, 18) in a trance or excess of mind, as he calleth it, Act. 22, 17. It was written at Troas (it is thought) and sent by Titus, as we read chap. 8.
\ip It is for the most part against those false Apostles whom in the first part of the first to the Corinthians, he noted, or rather spared, but now is constrained to deal openly against them, & to defend both his own person which they sought to bring into contempt, making way thereby to the correption of the Corinthians, and withal to maintain the excellency of the Ministry and Ministers of the new Testament, above which they did magnify the Ministry of the old Testament: bearing themselves very high because they were Jews.
\ip Against these therefore S. Paul avoucheth the preeminent power of his Ministry, by which power also he giveth a pardon to the incestuous fornicator whom he excommunicated in the last Epistle, seeing now his penance, and again threateneth to come & excommunicate those that had grievously sinned and remained impenitent. Two chapters also he interposeth of the contributions to the Church of Hierusalem, mentioned in his last, exhorting them to do liberally, and also to have all in a readiness against his coming.
\c 1
\cd By his troubles in Asia he comforteth them: and (against his adversaries the false Apostles of the Jews) allegeth to them the testimony of his own and also of their conscience, 17. answering them that objected lightness against him, for not coming to Corinth according to his promise.
\p
\v 1 \sc Paul\sc* an Apostle of \sc Jesus\sc* Christ by the will of God, and Timothee our brother: to the Church of God that is at Corinth, with all the saints that are in all Achaia.
\v 2 Grace unto you and peace from God our Father, & from our Lord \sc Jesus\sc* Christ.
\v 3 \f 1 \fr 1.3 \ft The Epistle for S. Marcellus. Ian. \+it 16.\+it* and S. Linus Sept. \+it 23.\+it* And for some other Martyrs Bishops.\f* Blessed be the God and Father of our Lord \sc Jesus\sc* Christ, the Father of mercies and God of all comfort,
\v 4 who comforteth us in all our tribulation; that we also may be able to comfort them that are in all distress, \f 1 \fr 1.4 \ft \+it c διὰ τῆς παρακλήσεως ἕις παρακαλούμεθα.\+it*\f* by the exhortation wherewith we also are exhorted of God.
\v 5 For as the passions of Christ abound in us: so also by Christ doth our comfort abound. \ef - \fr 1.5 \fq Passions of Christ. \ft ¹ All the afflictions of the faithful be called Christ's own passions, not only because they be suffered for him, but for that there is so strait conjunction and communion betwixt him being the head, and every of the living members of his body, which is the Church, that whatsoever is suffered by any one of the same, is counted as a piece of his own Passion. As likewise whatsoever good works be done to any of them, or by any of them, be accepted as done to, or by Christ himself, Which thing if the Protestants well weighed, they would not marvel that the Catholic Church attributeth such force of ² merit and satisfaction to the work of holy men. \fq ¹ \ft All our afflictions, be Christ's afflictions, for the conjunction between the head & body. \fq ² \ft Merit and satisfaction.\ef* \ef - \fr 1.5 \fq The comfort abound. \ft ¹ Worldly men that see only the exterior miseries and afflictions that Catholics do suffer being persecuted by the Heathen or Heretics, deem them exceeding miserable. But if they felt or could conceive the aboundance of consolation which Christ ever giveth according to the measure of their afflictions, they would never wonder at the voluntary toleration of what torments soever for Christ's sake, but would wish rather themselves to be in any dungeon in England with the comfort that such have from God, than to live out of the Church in all the wealth of the world. \fq ¹ \ft Worldly men feel not the comfort of afflicted Catholics.\ef*
\v 6 And whether we be in tribulation, for your exhortation & salvation: whether we be exhorted, for your exhortation & salvation, which worketh the toleration of the same passions which we also do suffer:
\v 7 and our hope is firm for you: knowing that as you are partakers of the passions, so shall you be of the consolation also.
\v 8 For we will not have you ignorant, brethren, concerning our tribulation, which happened in Asia, that we were pressed above measure above our power, so that it was tedious unto us even to live.
\v 9 But we in ourselves, had the answer of death, that we be not trusting in ourselves, but in God who raiseth up the dead,
\v 10 who hath delivered and doth deliver us out of so great dangers: in whom we hope that he will yet also deliver us,
\v 11 you helping withal in prayer for us, that by many men's persons, thanks for that gift which is in us, may be given by many in our behalf. \ef - \fr 1.11 \fq You helping in prayer. \ft ¹ S. Paul knew that the help of other men's prayers was nothing derogatory to the office of Christ's mediation or intercession for him, nor to the hope that he had in God: and therefore he craveth the Corinthians' aid herein as a support and succours for himself in the sight of God. With what reason or Scripture then, can the Protestants say that the prayers of Saints be injurious to Christ, or not to stand with the confidence we have in him? As though it were more dishonour to God that we should use the aid of Saints in heaven, than of sinners in earth: or ² that the intercession of these our fellows beneath, were more available than the prayers of those that be in the glorious sight of God above. \fq ¹ \ft Intercession of Saints or holy men for us, no derogation to Christ. \fq ² \ft \+it Hiero. cont. Vigil.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 1.11 \fq By many men's. \ft ¹ He meaneth, that as the prayers of many joined together for him, shall be rather heard, than of any alone: so their common thanks-giving to God for granting their request, shall be more acceptable and glorious to God, than any one man's thanks alone. Which thing doth much commend the holy Church's public prayers, processions, stations, and pilgrimages, where so many meet and uniformly join their prayers and lauds together unto God. \fq ¹ \ft Public prayers & fasts.\ef*
\v 12 For our glory is this, the testimony of our conscience, that in simplicity and sincerity of God, and not in carnal wisdom, but in the grace of God we have conversed in this world: and more aboundantly towards you.
\v 13 For we write no other things to you than that you have read and know. And I hope that you shall know unto the end:
\v 14 as also you have known us in part, that we are your glory, as you also ours in the day of our Lord \sc Jesus\sc* Christ. \ef - \fr 1.14 \fq Your glory. \ft ¹ The Apostles, teachers, and preachers, that convert countries or particular persons to Christ, and the peoples or parties by them converted, shall in the day of judgement have much mutual joy and glory of and for each other, one giving to the other great matter of merit in this life, and of reward in the next. See \+it 1. Thess. 2. v. 19.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Their glory in heaven that convert others.\ef*
\v 15 And in this confidence I would first have come to you, that you might have a second grace:
\v 16 and by you pass into Macedonia, and again from Macedonia come to you, and of you be brought on my way into Jewry.
\v 17 Whereas then I was thus minded, did I use lightness? Or the things that I mind, do I mind according to the flesh, that there be with me, \it It is\it* and \it It is not?\it*
\v 18 But God is faithful, because our preaching which was to you, there is not in it, \it It is,\it* and, \it It is not.\it* \ef - \fr 1.18 \fq It is, it is not. \ft ¹ As he dischargeth himself of all other levity touching his promise or purpose of coming to them, so much more of all inconstancy in preaching Christ's doctrine and faith. Wherein, one day to affirm, another day to deny, to dissent from his fellows or from himself, to change every year or in every epistle the form of his former teaching, to come daily with new devices repugnant to his own rules, were not agreeable to an Apostle and true teacher of Christ, but proper to false prophets and Heretics. Whereof we have notorious examples in the Protestants: who being destitute of the spirit of peace, concord, constancy, unity, and verity, as they vary from their own writings which they retract, reform or deform continually, so both in their preachings, & form of Service, they are so restless, changeable and repugnant to themselves, that if they were not kept in awe with much ado, by temporal laws, or by the shame and rebuke of the world, they would coin us every year or every Parliament, new Communions, new faiths, and new Christs, as you see by the manifold endeavours of the Puritans. And this to be the proper note of false Apostles and Heretics, see in \+it S. Irenæus li. 1. c. 18.\+it* and \+it Tertull. de præscript. S. Basil. ep. 12.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft The Protestants' inconstancy in changing their writings, translations, service books, &c.\ef*
\v 19 For the Son of God \sc Jesus\sc* Christ, who by us was preached among you, by me and Sylvanus and Timothee, was not, \it It is,\it* and, \it It is not,\it* but, \it It is,\it* was in him.
\v 20 For all the promises of God that are, in him \it It is:\it* therefore also by him, Amen to God, unto our glory.
\v 21 And he that confirmeth us with you in Christ, and that hath anointed us, God:
\v 22 who also hath sealed us, and given the pledge of the Spirit in our hearts. \ef - \fr 1.22 \fq Hath sealed. \ft ¹ The learned Divines prove by this place and by the like in the fourth to the Ephesians ², that the Sacrament of Baptism doth not only give grace, but imprinteth and sealeth the soul of the baptized, with a spiritual sign, mark, badge, or token, which can never be blotted out, neither by sin, heresy, apostasy, nor other ways, but remaineth for ever in man for the cognisance of his Christendom, and for distinction from others which were never of Christ's fold. By which also he is as it were consecrated and deputed to God, made capable and partaker of the rights of the Church, and subject to her laws and discipline. See S. Hierom \+it in 4. Ephes.\+it* S. Ambrose \+it li. 1 de Sp. Sancto cap. 6.\+it* S. Cyril Hierosol. \+it Catechesi. 17\+it* at the end, and S. Dionysius Areopag. \+it c. 2. Eccl. Hierarch.\+it* The which Fathers express that spiritual sign by diverse agreeable names, which the Church and most Divines, after S. Augustine, call the \+it character\+it* of Baptism. By the truth and force of which spiritual note or mark of the soul, he specially convinceth the Donatists, that the said Sacrament though given and ministered by Heretics or Schismatics or who else so ever, can never be reiterated. See \+it ep. 57. & li. 6 cont. Donatist. cap. 1. & li. 2 cont. Parmenianum c. 13.\+it* As the like indelible characters given also by the Sacraments of Confirmation and Orders, do make those also irreiterable and never to be received but once. Whereas all other Sacraments saving these three, may be often received of the selfsame person. And that Holy Orders cannot be iterated, see S. Augustine \+it li. 2 cont. Parmen. c. 13. li. de bono coniug. c. 24.\+it* and S. Gregory \+it li. 2 Regist. ep. 32.\+it* The like of Confirmation is decreed in the most ancient Councel Tarracon. \+it cap. 6.\+it* ³ Finally that this character is given only by these said three Sacraments, and is the cause that none of them can be in any man repeated or reiterated, see the decrees of the Councels Florentine and Trent. Which yet is no new device of them, as the Heretics falsely affirm, but agreeable (as you see) both to the Scriptures and also to the ancient fathers and Councels. \fq ¹ \ft The indelible character of Baptism, Confirmation, Holy Orders. \fq ² \ft \+it Eph. 4, 30.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it See Conc. Tarraco. to. 2. Concil.\+it*\ef*
\v 23 And I call God to witness upon my soul, that sparing you, I came not any more to Corinth, not because we over-rule your faith: but, we are helpers of your joy. For in the faith you stand. \ef - \fr 1.23 \fq Not because we over-rule. \ft ¹ Calvin and his seditious Sectaries with other like \+it which despise dominion,\+it* as S. Jude describeth such, would by this place deliver themselves from all yoke of spiritual Magistrates and Rulers: namely that they be subject to no man touching their faith, or for the examination and trial of their doctrine, but to God and his word only. And no marvel that the malefactors and rebels of the Church would come to no tribunal but God's, that so they may remain unpunished at least during this life. For though the Scriptures plainly condemn their heresies, yet they could writhe themselves out by false glosses, constructions, corruptions, and denials of the books to be Canonical, if there were no laws or judicial sentence of men to rule and repress them. ² Notwithstanding then these words of S. Paul, whereby only tyrannical, insolent, and proud behaviour and indiscreet rigor of Prelates or Apostles towards their flocks is noted, as also in the first of S. Peter \+it cap. 5.\+it* (the Greek word in these places ³, and in the Gospel \+it Mt. 20, 25. Mr. 10, 42.\+it* signifying lordly and insolent dominion:) yet he had and exercised just rule, preeminence, and prelacy over them, not only for their life, but also and principally touching their faith. For he might & did call them to account for the same, and excommunicated heretics for forsaking their faith. \+it 1 Cor. 4. 5. 2 Cor. 10, 4. 13, 10. 1 Tim. 1, 20. Tit. 1, 11.\+it* And all Christian men are bound to obey their lawful Prelates in matters of faith and doctrine specially, and must not under that ridiculous pretence of obeying God's word only (which is the shift of all other Heretics, as Anabaptists, Arians, and the like, as well as the Protestants) disobey God's Church, Councels, and their own Pastors and Bishops, who by the Scriptures have the regiment of their souls, and may examine and punish as well John Calvin as Simon Magus, for falling from the Catholic faith. For though God alone be the Lord, author and giver of faith, yet they are his ⁴ cooperators and coadjutors by whom the faithful do believe and be preserved in the true faith, and be defended from wolves, which be Heretics, seeking to corrupt them in the same. And this same Apostle ⁵ challengeth to be their father as he that begat and formed them by his preaching in Christ. \fq ¹ \ft The Calvinists will be subject to no tribunal in earth, for trial of their religion. \fq ² \ft Tyrannical dominion is forbid in Prelates, not Ecclesiastical Sovereignty for examination of faith or manners. \fq ³ \ft \+it κατακυριεύειν.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft \+it συνεργοὶ 1. Cor. 3, 9.\+it* \fq ⁵ \ft \+it 1. Cor. 4, 15.\+it*\ef*
\c 2
\cd Prosecuting the true cause which in the last chapter he gave of his not coming, 6. he pardoneth now after some part of penance, him that for incest he excommunicated in the last epistle, requiring them obediently to consent thereunto. 12. Then of his going from Troas in to Macedonia; God every-where giving him the triumph.
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* I have determined with my-self this same thing, not to come to you again in sorrow.
\v 2 For if I make you sorry; & who is it that can make me glad, but he that is made sorry by me?
\v 3 And this same I wrote to you; that I may not, when I come, have sorrow upon sorrow, of the which I ought to rejoice: trusting in you all, that my joy is the joy of you all.
\v 4 For of much tribulation and anguish of heart I wrote to you by many tears: not that you should be made sorry; but that you may know what charity I have more aboundantly toward you.
\v 5 And if any man hath made sorrowful, not me hath he made sorrowful, but in part, that I burden not all you.
\v 6 To him that is such a one, this rebuke sufficeth that is given of many: \ef - \fr 2.6 \fq This rebuke sufficeth. \ft ¹ This Corinthian for incest was excommunicated and put to penance by the Apostle, as appeareth in the former Epistle c. 5. And here order is given for his absolution and pardoning. Where in first we have a plain example and proof of the Apostolic power, there of binding, and here of loosing: there of punishing, here of pardoning: there of retaining sins, here of remission. Secondly we may hereby prove that not only amendment, ceasing to sin, or repentance in heart and before God alone, is always enough to obtain full reconcilement, whereas we see here his separation also from the faithful, and the Sacraments, and from all company or dealing with other Christian men, besides other bodily affliction: all which, called of the Apostle before ² \+it interitus carnis, the destruction of the flesh\+it*, and named here, \+it Rebuke\+it*, or (as the ³ Greek word also importeth) mulct, penalty, correction, chastisement, were enjoined him by the Apostle's commandment in the face of the Church, and by the offender patiently sustained so long. ⁴ Thirdly we see that it lieth in the hands of the Apostles, Bishops, and spiritual Magistrates, to measure the time of such penance or discipline, not only according to the weight of the offence committed, but also according to the weakness of the persons punished, and other respects, of time and place, as to their wisdom shall be thought most agreeable to the party's good, and the Church's edification. ⁵ Lastly by this whole handling of the offender's case, we may refute the wicked heresy of the Protestants, that would make the simple believe, no punishment of a man's own person for sins committed, nor penance enjoined by the Church, nor any pains temporal or satisfaction for our life past, to be necessary, but all such things to be superfluous, because Christ hath satisfied enough for all. Which Epicurean doctrine is refelled, not only hereby, but also by the Prophets' ⁶, John the Baptist's ⁷, Christ's, and the Apostles' preaching ⁸ of penance and condign works or fruits of repentance, to every man in his own person, and not in Christ's person only: and by the whole life and most plain speeches and penitential canons of the holy doctors and Councels prescribing times of penance, commanding penance, enjoining penance, and continually using the word satisfaction in this case throughout all their works, as our Adversaries themselves cannot but confess. \fq ¹ \ft The Apostle excommunicateth, enjoineth penance: and afterward pardoneth & absolveth. \fq ² \ft \+it 1. Cor. 5, 5.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it ἐπιτιμία.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft Pardon or remission of penance enjoined. \fq ⁵ \ft Penance and satisfaction evidently proved against the Protestants. \fq ⁶ \ft \+it Ioel, 2, 12.\+it* \fq ⁷ \ft \+it Mt. 3. & 4.\+it* \fq ⁸ \ft \+it Act. 2. & 26.\+it*\ef*
\v 7 so that contrarywise you should rather pardon and comfort him, lest perhaps such an one be swallowed up with over great sorrow.
\v 8 For the which cause I beseech you that you confirm charity toward him. \ef - \fr 2.8 \fq I beseech you. \ft ¹ They which at the beginning did bear too much with the offender, and seemed loath to have him excommunicated in so austere manner: yet through their obedience to the Apostle became on the other side so rigorous, and so far detested the malefactor after he was excommunicated, that the Apostle now meaning to absolve him, was glad to entreat and command them also to accept him to their company and grace again. \fq ¹ \ft Zeal against the excommunicate.\ef*
\v 9 For therefore also have I written that I may know the experiment of you, whether in all things you be obedient. \ef - \fr 2.9 \fq Obedient. \ft ¹ Though in the last chapter he discharged himself of tyrannical dominion over them, yet he challengeth their obedience in all things as their Pastor and Superior, and consequently in this point of receiving to mercy the penitent Corinthian. Whereby we see, that as the power and authority of excommunicating, so of absolving also was in S. Paul's person, though both were to be done in the face of the Church: else he would not have commanded or required their obedience. \fq ¹ \ft The Apostle challengeth their obedience to his Ecclesiastical authority.\ef*
\v 10 And whom you have pardoned any thing, I also. For, myself also that which \f 1 \fr 2.10 \ft \+it c κεχάρισμαι.\+it* Though he did great Penance (saith Theodorete) yet he calleth this pardoning. \+it χάριν, a grace,\+it* because his sin was\f* I pardoned, if I pardoned any thing, for you in the person of Christ, \ef - \fr 2.10 \fq I also. \ft ¹ The Heretics and others not well founded in the Scriptures and antiquity, marvel at the Pope's pardons, counting them either fruitless or unlawful or no elder than S. Gregory. But indeed the authority, power, and right of them is of Christ's own word and commission, principally given to Peter, and so afterward to all the Apostles, and in their persons to all the chief Pastors of the Church, when it was said, ² \+it Whatsoever you loose in earth, shall be loosed in heaven.\+it* By which commission the holy Bishops of old did cut off large pieces of penance enjoined to offenders, and gave peace, grace, or indulgence, ³ before they had accomplished the measure of their appointed or deserved punishment. And that is to give pardon. And so S. Paul here did towards the Corinthian, whom he assoiled of mere grace and mercy, as the word \+it donare\+it* or \+it condonare\+it* doth signify ⁴, when he might longer have kept him in penance and temporal affliction for his offence. Whereof though he had already before God inwardly repented, yet was he justly holden under this correction for some satisfaction of his fault past, during the Apostle's pleasure. ⁵ To remit then the temporal punishment or chastisement due to sinners after the offence itself and the guilt thereof be forgiven of God, is an Indulgence or pardon. Which the principal Magistrates of God's Church by Christ's warrant and the Apostle's example, have ever done, being no less authorised to pardon than to punish, and by imitation of our Master (who forgave ⁶ the adulteress and diverse other offenders, not only their sins, but also often the temporal punishments due for the same) are as much given to mercy as to justice. \fq ¹ \ft The authority of Indulgences whereupon it is grounded. \fq ² \ft \+it Mat. 18, 18.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it Cypria. ep. 13. 14. 15.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft \+it κεχάρισμαι. See Theodoret in hunc loc.\+it* \fq ⁵ \ft What is a pardon or indulgence. \fq ⁶ \ft \+it Io. 8, 11.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 2.10 \fq For you. \ft ¹ ² Theodorete upon this place saith that the Apostle gave this pardon to the Corinthian at the intercession of the blessed men Timotheus and Titus. And we may read in sundry places, of S. Cyprian ³ namely, that Indulgences or remissions were given in the primitive Church by the mediation of holy Confessors or Martyrs, and by communicating the satisfactory works of one to another, to which end they gave their letters to Bishops in the behalf of diverse their Christian brethren, a thing most agreeable to the mutual intercourse that is between the members of Christ's mystical body, and very answerable to God's justice, ⁴ which by supply of the one sort that aboundeth, standeth entire in respect of the other sort also that wanteth. In which kind the Apostle confesseth that himself by his suffering and tribulations, supplieth the wants of such passions as Christ hath to suffer ⁵, not in his own person, but in his body, which is his Church. Whereupon we infer most assuredly, that the satisfactory and penal works of holy Saints suffered in this life, be communicable and applicable to the use of other faithful men their fellow-members in our Lord, and to be dispensed according to every one's necessity and deserving, by them whom Christ hath constituted over his family, and hath made the dispensers of his treasures. \fq ¹ \ft Indulgences or pardons in the primitive Church. \fq ² \ft \+it Theodor. in hunc locum.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it Cypr. locis citatis.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft \+it 2. cor. 8.\+it* \fq ⁵ \ft \+it Col. 1, 24.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 2.10 \fq In the person of Christ. \ft ¹ For that many might of ignorance or pride reprove the practice of God's Church and her Officers, or deny the Apostle's authority to be so great over men's souls as to punish and pardon in this sort, S. Paul doth purposely and precisely tell them that he doth give pardon as Christ's Vicar, or as bearing his person in this case: and therefore that no man may marvel of his power herein, except he think that Christ's power, authority, and commission is not sufficient to release temporal punishment due to sinners. And this to be the proper meaning of these words, ² \+it In the person of Christ\+it*, and not as the Protestants would have it ³ (the better to avoid the former conclusion of the Apostle's giving indulgence) \+it In the face or sight of Christ\+it*, you may easily understand by the Apostle's like insinuation of Christ's power, when he committed this offender to Satan, affirming that he gave that sentence in the name and with the ⁴ \+it virtue\+it* or \+it power of our Lord \+sc Jesus Christ\+sc*\+it*. In all which cases the Protestants' blindness is exceeding great, who cannot see that this is not the way to extol Christ's power, to deny it to his Priests, seeing the Apostle challengeth it by that that Christ hath such power, & that himself doth it in his name, virtue, and person. So now in this, and in no other name, give Popes and Bishops their pardons. Which pertaining properly to releasing only of temporal punishment due after the sin and the eternal punishment be forgiven, is not so great a matter as the remission of the sin itself: which yet the Priests ⁵ by express commission do also remit. \fq ¹ \ft All pardon and remission is in the virtue and name of Christ. \fq ² \ft \+it ἐν προσώπῳ Χριστοῦ.\+it* \fq ³ \ft Heretical translation. \fq ⁴ \ft \+it 1. Cor. 5, 4.\+it* \fq ⁵ \ft \+it Io. 20, 23.\+it*\ef*
\v 11 that we be not circumvented of Satan. For we are not ignorant of his cogitations. \ef - \fr 2.11 \fq Circumvented of Satan. \ft ¹ We may see hereby, that the dispensation of such discipline, and the releasing of the same, be put into the power and hands of God's ministers, to deal more or less rigorously, to pardon sooner or later, punish longer or shorter while, as shall be thought best to their wisdom. For the end of all such correction or pardoning must be the salvation of the party's soul, as the Apostle noted \+it 1 Cor. 5, 5.\+it* Which to some, and some certain times, may be better procured by rigour of discipline than by indulgence, to some others, by lenity and humane dealing (so pardoning of penance is called in old Councels ² ) rather than by overmuch chastisement. For consideration whereof, in some ages of the Church, much discipline, ³ great penance and satisfaction was both enjoined and also willingly sustained, and then was the less pardoning and fewer indulgences, because in that voluntary use and acceptation of punishment, and great zeal and fervour of spirit, every man fulfilled his penance, and few asked pardon. Now in the fall of devotion and loathsomeness that men commonly have to do great penance, though the sins be far greater than ever before, ⁴ yet our holy mother the Church knowing with the Apostle the cogitations of Satan, how he would in this delicate time, drive men either to desperation, or to forsake Christ and his Church and all hope of salvation, rather than they would enter into the course of canonical discipline, enjoineth small penance, and seldom useth extremity with offenders as the holy Bishops of the primitive Church did, but condescending to the weakness of her children, pardoneth exceeding often and much, not only all enjoined penance, but also all or great parts of what punishment temporal soever due or deserved, either in this world or in the next. As for the Heretics which neither like the Church's lenity and pardoning in these days, nor the old rigor of the primitive Church, they be like to the Jews ⁵ that condemned John the Baptist of austerity, and Christ of too much freedom and liberty: not knowing nor liking indeed either Christ's ordinance and commission in binding or loosing, or his providence in the government of the Church. \fq ¹ \ft All binding & loosing must be used to the party's salvation. \fq ² \ft \+it Conc. Nic. can. 12. Ancyran. can. 2. & 5.\+it* \fq ³ \ft The great penance of the primitive Church. \fq ⁴ \ft Why more pardons and Indulgences now than in old time. \fq ⁵ \ft \+it Mat. 11, 18.\+it*\ef*
\v 12 And when I was come to Troas for the Gospel of Christ, and a door was opened unto me in our Lord,
\v 13 I had no rest in my spirit, for that I found not Titus my brother, but bidding them fare well, I went forth into Macedonia.
\v 14 And thanks be to God, who always triumpheth us in Christ \sc Jesus\sc*, and manifesteth the odour of his knowledge by us in every place.
\v 15 For we are the good odour of Christ unto God in them that are saved, & in them that perish.
\v 16 To some indeed the odour of death unto death: but to others the odour of life unto life. And to these things who is so sufficient?
\v 17 For we are not as very many, adulterating the word of God, but of sincerity, and as of God, before God, in Christ we speak. \ef - \fr 2.17 \fq Adulterating. \ft ¹ The Greek word ² signifieth to make commodity of the word of God, as vulgar Vintners do of their wine. Whereby is expressed the peculiar trade of all Heretics, and \fq ¹ \ft The Heretics' corrupting of the \fq ² \ft \+it καπηλεύοντες.\+it*\ef*
\c 3
\cd Lest the Judaical false Apostles should object again that he praiseth himself, he saith that the Corinthians are his commendation: and they in their hearts being justified by his ministry, he thereof inferreth that the Ministers of the new Testament are far more glorious than they of the old, 12. and our people more lightened than theirs.
\p
\v 1 \sc Begin\sc* we again to commend our-selves? or do we need (as certain) epistles of commendation to you, of from you?
\v 2 Our epistle you are, written in our hearts, which is known & read of all men:
\v 3 being manifested that you are the epistle of Christ, ministered by us, & written not with ink, but with the Spirit of the living God: not in tables of stone, but in the tables carnal of the heart. \ef - \fr 3.3 \fq The Epistle of Christ. \ft ¹ S. Paul and other holy writers of Scriptures did set down many things in writing, by pen, ink, and paper, all which be of the Holy Ghost: but the special and proper book of Christ's truth and Gospel, is not the external writing in those dead creatures, but in the hearts of the faithful, being the proper subject of these truths and graces preached in the new Testament, and the habitacle of the Holy Ghost. In the which book of faithful men's hearts S. Paul wrote diverse things not uttered in any Epistle: as sundry of the Apostles wrote the Christian religion in the hearts of their hearers only, and in other material books not at all. Whereof S. Irenæus \+it li. 3. c. 4.\+it* saith, ² \+it What and if the Apostles also had left no Scriptures, ought we not to follow the order of the tradition, which they delivered unto them to whom they committed the Churches? To the which ordinance many nations of those barbarous people that have believed in Christ, do consent, without letter or ink, having salvation written in their hearts, and keeping diligently the tradition of the elders.\+it* And S. Hierom. \+it (cont. Io. Hieros. c. 9. ad Pam.) In the Creed of our faith and hope, which being delivered by tradition from the Apostles, is not written in paper and ink, but in the tables carnal of the heart\+it*. And this is the Church's book also, whereby and wherein she keepeth faithfully all truth written in the hearts of those to whom the Apostles did preach, with the like diligence as she keepeth and preserveth the other book which is of holy Scriptures, from all corruption of Heretics and other injuries. \fq ¹ \ft The Apostles wrote the Gospel in men's hearts much more than in paper. \fq ² \ft Scripture written, and Tradition unwritten.\ef*
\v 4 \f 1 \fr 3.4 \ft The Epistle upon the 12. Sunday after Pentecost.\f* And such confidence we have by Christ to God:
\v 5 not that we be sufficient to think any thing of our-selves, as of our-selves: but our sufficiency is of God. \ef - \fr 3.5 \fq Of ourselves. \ft ¹ This maketh first against the Heretics called Pelagians, that hold our meritorious actions or cogitations to be of free-will only, and not of God's special grace. Secondly against the Protestants, who on the contrary side refer all to God, and take away man's freedom and proper motion in his thoughts and doings: the Apostle confessing our good cogitations to be our own, but not as coming of ourselves, but of God. \fq ¹ \ft God's grace & free will both must concur.\ef*
\v 6 Who also hath made us meet ministers of the new testament: not in the letter, but in the Spirit. For the letter killeth: but the Spirit quickeneth. \ef - \fr 3.6 \fq The letter killeth. \ft ¹ As the letter of the old Law not truly understood, nor referred to Christ, commanding and not giving grace and spirit to fulfill that which was commanded, did by occasion kill the carnal Jew: so the letter of the new Testament not truly taken nor expounded by the Spirit of Christ (which is only in his Church) killeth the Heretic: who also being carnal and void of spirit, gaineth nothing by the external precepts or good lessons of the Scriptures, but rather taketh hurt by the same. See S. Augustine \+it to. 10. Ser. 70. & 100. de tempore. & li. de Sp. & lit. c. 5. 6. & seq.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft The letter killeth both Jew and Heretic.\ef*
\v 7 And if the ministration of death with letters figured in stones, was in glory, so that the children of Israel could not \f 1 \fr 3.7 \ft The Apostles wrote the Gospel in men's hearts much more than in paper.\f* behold the face of Moyses for the glory of his countenance, that is made void;
\v 8 how shall not the ministration of the Spirit be more in glory?
\v 9 For if the ministration of damnation be in glory, much more the ministry of justice aboundeth in glory. \ef - \fr 3.9 \fq Much more. \ft ¹ The preeminence of the new Testament and of the priesthood or Ministry thereof before the old, is, that the new, by all her Sacraments and Priests as ministers immediate of grace and remission of sins, doth so \+it ex opere operato\+it* give the spirit of life and charity into the hearts of the faithful, as the old did give the letter or external act of the Law. \fq ¹ \ft The preeminence of the new Testament, Sacraments, &c.\ef*
\v 10 For neither was it glorified, which in this part was glorious, by reason of the excelling glory.
\v 11 For if that which is made void, is by glory: much more that which abideth, is in glory.
\v 12 Having therefore such hope, we use much confidence:
\v 13 and not \x - \xt Exo. 34, 33.\x* as Moyses put a veil upon his face, that the children of Israel might not behold his face, which is made void.
\v 14 but their senses were dulled. For until this present day, the self-same veil in the lecture of the old testament remaineth unrevealed (because in Christ it is made void) \ef - \fr 3.14 \fq The self-same veil. \ft ¹ As the Jews reading the Old Testament, by reason of their blindness (which God for the punishment of their incredulity suffereth to remain as a cover upon their eyes and hearts) cannot see Christ in the Scriptures which they daily hear read in their Synagogues, but shall, when they believe in him and have the cover removed, perceive all to be most plainly done and spoken of him in their law & Scriptures: even so Heretics having (as S. Augustine ² noteth) a far greater cover of blindness and incredulity over their hearts in respect of the Catholic Church which they impugn, than the Jews have concerning Christ, cannot see, though they read or hear the Scriptures read never so much, the marvelous evidence of the Catholic Church and truth in all points: but when they shall return again to the obedience of the same Church, they shall find the Scriptures most clear for her and her doctrine, and shall wonder at their former blindness. \fq ¹ \ft The heretics more blind in not seeing the Church, than the Jews in not seeing Christ. \fq ² \ft \+it Aug. in Psal. 30. Conc. 2.\+it*\ef*
\v 15 but until this present day, when Moyses is read, a veil is put upon their heart.
\v 16 But when he shall be converted to our Lord, the veil shall be taken away.
\v 17 And \x - \xt Io. 4, 24.\x* our Lord is a Spirit. And where the Spirit of our Lord is, there is liberty. \ef - \fr 3.17 \fq Liberty. \ft ¹ The Spirit and grace of God in the new Testament dischargeth us of the bondage of the Law and sin, but is not a warrant to us of fleshly licence, as S. Peter writeth ²: nor dischargeth Christians of their obedience to order, law, and power of Magistrates spiritual or temporal, as some Heretics of these days do seditiously teach. \fq ¹ \ft True Christian liberty. \fq ² \ft \+it 1. Pet. 2, 16.\+it*\ef*
\v 18 But we all, beholding the glory of our Lord with face revealed, are transformed into the same image from glory unto glory, as of our Lord's Spirit.
\c 4
\cd That according as so glorious a ministry requireth, he liveth and preacheth sincerely, 7. the which glory his Adversaries cannot count vain, considering his persecutions: because persecution is to God's glory, and to our humility and hope, and meritorious of increase of grace in this life, and of most glorious bodies and souls afterward.
\p
\v 1 \sc Therefore\sc* having this ministration; according as we have obtained mercy, we fail not,
\v 2 but we renounce the secret things of dishonesty, not walking in craftiness, nor adulterating the word of God, but in manifestation of the truth commending our-selves to every conscience of men before God. \ef - \fr 4.2 \fq Adulterating. \ft ¹ ² He giveth often warning of false teachers, whose special and proper study is to falsify and adulterate by deceitful constructions, interpretations, and applications, the word of God: having no other end but to make their advantage of the Scriptures, and to gain glory and estimation among the sinful and simple, by new devised expositions. Wherein the Protestants do excel the ancient Heretics, none ever more impurely handling the word of God than they do. Origen ³ calleth such \+it Scripturarum fures & adulteros\+it*, thieves and adulterers of the Scriptures. S. Cyprian \+it (de unit. Ec. nu. 7.)\+it* calleth them, corrupters of the Gospel, false interpreters, artificers and crafts-masters in corrupting the truth. On the other side, for special reverence and sincerity of dealing in those matters, the fathers and all Catholic preachers or Expositors were of old called according to S. Paul's words to Timothee ⁴, \+it Recte tractantes verbum Dei\+it*, right handlers of the word of God. \fq ¹ \ft Heretics corrupters of God's word; Catholic Doctors, right handlers thereof. \fq ² \ft \+it See Iren. li. 1. c. 1.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it in 2. ad Rom.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft \+it 2. Tim. 2.\+it*\ef*
\v 3 And if our Gospel be also hid, in them that perish it is hid,
\v 4 in whom the God of this world hath blinded the minds of the infidels, that the illumination of the Gospel of the glory of Christ, who is the image of God, might not shine to them.
\v 5 \f 1 \fr 4.5 \ft The Epistle for S. Athanasius. \+it Mai. 2.\+it*\f* For we preach not our-selves, but \sc Jesus\sc* Christ our Lord: and us, your servants by \sc Jesus\sc*:
\v 6 because God that commanded light to shine of darkness, he hath shined in our hearts to the illumination of the knowledge of the glory of God, in the face of Christ \sc Jesus\sc*.
\v 7 But we have this treasure in earthen vessels, that the excellency may be of the power of God, and not of us.
\v 8 In all things we suffer tribulation, but are not in distress: \f 1 \fr 4.8 \ft I \+it c aporiamur. See S. Ambr. Theoph.\+it*\f* we want, but are not destitute:
\v 9 we suffer persecution, but are not forsaken: we are cast down, but we perish not:
\v 10 always bearing about in our body the mortification of \sc Jesus\sc*, that the life also of \sc Jesus\sc* may be manifested in our bodies.
\v 11 For we that live, are always delivered unto death for \sc Jesus\sc*: that the life also of \sc Jesus\sc* may be manifested in our mortal flesh.
\v 12 Death then worketh in us, but life in you.
\v 13 And having the same spirit of faith, as it is written: \x - \xt Ps. 115, 10.\x* \it I believed, for the which cause I have spoken,\it* we also believe, for the which cause we speak also:
\v 14 knowing that he which raised up \sc Jesus\sc*, will raise up us also with \sc Jesus\sc* and set us with you.
\v 15 For all things are for you: that the grace abounding by many in giving of thanks, may abound unto the glory of God.
\v 16 For which cause we fail not: but although that our man which is without, \f 1 \fr 4.16 \ft \+it b corrumpitur.\+it*\f* corrupt: yet that which is within, is renewed from day to day.
\v 17 For that our tribulation which presently is momentary & light, \f 1 \fr 4.17 \ft c The English Bible 1577, doth falsely translate, \+it prepareth.\+it*\f*\f 2 \fr 4.17 \ft \+it c κατεργάζεται.\+it*\f* worketh above measure exceedingly an eternal weight of glory in us, \ef - \fr 4.17 \fq Worketh. \ft ¹ The temporal and short tribulations which we patiently and willingly suffer for Christ, do win us everlasting joy and glory. And it is here to be noted against the Heretics, that tribulations do work or cause the said salvation, which they deny to be given for such things, but for or by faith only. S. Augustine ² maketh such tribulations for Christ so much the meritorious cause of everlasting life and rest, that he saith it is salable and bought thereby. And it is written. Sap. 10, \+it God rendereth or repayeth to just men the hire of their labours.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Tribulations meritorious of glory. \fq ² \ft \+it Aug. in Ps. 93. prope finem.\+it*\ef*
\v 18 we not considering the things that are seen, but that are not seen. For the things that be seen, are temporal: but those that be not seen, are eternal.
\c 5
\cd That after death of the body the soul may go to heaven: therefore, although naturally we abhor death, by grace he desireth it rather: 9. in consideration of Christ's just judgement, living as in the sight of God, yea and of their consciences: 12. Which he speaketh not to praise himself, but because of his Adversaries who did glory in carnal respects: but he and the other Apostles regard nothing but their reconciliation unto God by Christ, and to reconcile others also, as being his Legates for that purpose.
\p
\v 1 \sc For\sc* we know that if our earthly house of this habitation be dissolved, that we have a building of God, a house not made with hand, eternal in Heaven.
\v 2 For in this also do we groan, desirous to be over-clothed with our habitation that is from Heaven:
\v 3 yet so, if we be found clothed, not naked.
\v 4 For we also that are in this tabernacle, groan being burdened: because we would not be spoiled, but over-clothed, that that which is mortal might be swallowed up of life.
\v 5 And he that maketh us of this same, is God, who hath given us the pledge of the Spirit.
\v 6 Being bold therefore always, and knowing that while we are in the body, we are pilgrims from God,
\v 7 (for we walk by faith and not by sight)
\v 8 but we are bold, and have a good will to be pilgrims rather from the body, & \f 1 \fr 5.8 \ft This place proveth that the Saints departed now since Christ, sleep not till the day of judgement, and that they be not holden in any several place of rest from the fruition of God till the resurrection of their bodies, but that they be present with God in their souls.\f* to be present with our Lord.
\v 9 And therefore we endeavour, whether absent or present, to please him.
\v 10 For \x - \xt Ro. 14, 10.\x* we must all be manifested before the judgement seat of Christ, that every one may receive the proper things of the body, according as he hath done, either good or evil. \ef - \fr 5.10 \fq The proper things of his body. \ft ¹ S. Augustine \+it (Enchirid. c. 110.)\+it* objecteth this speech of the Apostle, as in the person of such as deny the prayers, alms, and sacrifices of the living to be available for the dead, and he answereth as followeth. \+it This practice\+it* (saith he) \+it of God's Church in the commendation of the dead, is nothing repugnant to the sentence of the Apostle, where he saith, that we shall all stand before the judgement seat of Christ, that every one may receive according to his deserts in the body, either good or evil. For, in his life and before death he deserved this, that these works after his death might be profitable unto him. For indeed they be not profitable for all men. And why so? But because of the difference and diversity of men's lives whiles they were in flesh.\+it* The like he hath in diverse other places. August. li. de Præd. Sanct. c. 12. & ad Dulcit. q. 2. \+it And so hath S. Denys\+it* c. 7. Ec. Hierarch. \fq ¹ \ft The objection against prayers for the dead, answered by S. Augustine.\ef* \ef - \fr 5.10 \fq Either good or evil. \ft ¹ Heaven is as well the reward of good works, as Hell is the stipend of ill works. Neither is faith alone sufficient to procure salvation, nor lack of faith the only cause of damnation: by good deeds men merit the one, and by ill deeds they deserve the other. This is the Apostle's doctrine here and in other places, howsoever the Adversaries of good life and works teach otherwise. \fq ¹ \ft Works meritorious and demeritorious.\ef*
\v 11 Knowing therefore the fear of our Lord we use persuasion to men: but to God we are manifest. And I hope also that in your consciences we are manifest.
\v 12 We commend not ourselves again to you, but give you occasion to glory for us: that you may have against them that glory in face, and not in heart.
\v 13 For whether we exceed in mind, to God: or whether we be sober, to you.
\v 14 For the charity of Christ urgeth us; judging this, that if one died for all, then all were dead.
\v 15 And Christ died for all: that they also which live, may not now live to themselves, but to him that died for them and rose again.
\v 16 Therefore we from hence-forth know no man according to the flesh. And if we have known Christ according to the flesh: but now we know him no more.
\v 17 If then any be in Christ a new creature: the old are passed, behold \x - \xt Esa. 43. 19. Apoc. 21, 5.\x* all things are made new.
\v 18 But all of God, who hath reconciled us to himself by Christ: and hath given us the ministry of reconciliation. \ef - \fr 5.18 \fq The ministry of reconciliation. \ft ¹ Christ is the chief Minister, according to his manhood, of all our reconcilement to God: and for him, as his ministers, the Apostles and their successors the Bishops and Priests of his Church, in whom the word of reconcilement, as well by ministering of the Sacrifice and Sacraments for remission of sins, as by preaching and government of the world to salvation, is placed. And therefore their preaching must be to us, as if Christ himself did preach: their absolution and remission of sins, as Christ's own pardon: their whole office being nothing else (as we see by this passage) but the Vicarship of Christ. \fq ¹ \ft Bishops and Priests under Christ, Ministers of our reconciliation.\ef*
\v 19 For God indeed was in Christ reconciling the world to himself, not imputing to them their sins, and hath put in us the word of reconciliation.
\v 20 For Christ therefore we are legates, God as it were exhorting by us. For Christ we beseech you, be reconciled to God.
\v 21 Him that knew no sin, for us he made \f 1 \fr 5.21 \ft c This is to say, a sacrifice and an host for sin. \+it See the last annot. of this chapter.\+it*\f* sin: that we might be made the justice of God in him. \ef - \fr 5.21 \fq The justice of God. \ft \+it Even as\+it* (saith S. Augustine) \+it when we read, Salvation is our Lord's, it is not meant that salvation whereby our Lord is saved, but whereby they are saved whom he saveth: so when it is said, God's justice, that is not to be understood wherewith God is just, but that wherewith men are just whom by his grace he justifieth.\+it* ¹ See S. Augustine \+it de Sp. & lit. c. 18. & ep. 120. ad Honoratum.\+it* and abhor Calvin's wicked and unlearned glosses on this place, that teacheth justice no otherwise to be in man, than sin in Christ. Whereas the Scriptures call man just, because ² \+it he doth\+it* justice: but not so call they Christ sin, because he doth sin, but because he taketh away sin, and is a sacrifice for sin, as the Heretics know very well, that know the use and signification of the Hebrew word ³ in all the old Testament, namely \+it Psal. 39, 8.\+it* and in the book of \+it Leviticus\+it* very often, \+it c. 5. 6. 9. 12. 14. 16.\+it* and \+it Numer. c. 29.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft God's justice, wherewith he maketh us just. \fq ² \ft \+it 1. Io. 3, 7.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it חטאת\+it*\ef*
\c 6
\cd That he helpeth with his exhortations, and in all things behaveth himself as becometh a Minister of God. 11. Which he speaketh so openly, because his heart is open unto them: exhorting them to be likewise open-hearted towards him, 14. and to avoid those infidels.
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* \f 1 \fr 6.1 \ft The Epistle upon the first Sunday of Lent.\f* we helping do exhort, that you receive not the grace of God in vain. \ef - \fr 6.1 \fq Helping. \ft ¹ For that he declared before the Ministers of the new Testament to be Christ's deputies, and that when they preach or do any function, God as it were speaketh or doeth it by them, he boldly now saith, \+it Helping therefore:\+it* ² that is to say, joining or working together with God, we do exhort. \fq ¹ \ft God's Ministers are his coadjutors. \fq ² \ft \+it συνεργοῦντες.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 6.1 \fq Grace in vain. \ft ¹ The grace of God worketh not in man against his will, nor forceth any thing without his acceptation and consent: and therefore it lieth in man's will to frustrate or to follow the motion of God, as this text plainly proveth. \fq ¹ \ft God's grace forceth no man against his will.\ef*
\v 2 (For he saith: \x - \xt Esa. 49, 8.\x* \it In time accepted have I heard thee; and in the day of salvation have I holpen thee.\it* Behold, now is the time acceptable: behold now the day of salvation.)
\v 3 to no man giving any offence, that our ministry be not blamed:
\v 4 \f 1 \fr 6.4 \ft The Epistle for many Martyrs.\f* but in all things let us exhibit ourselves as the ministers of God, in much patience, in tribulations, in necessities, in distresses,
\v 5 in stripes, in prisons, in seditions, in labours, in watchings, in fastings, \ef - \fr 6.5 \fq In watchings. \ft ¹ When in the midst of many miseries and persecutions, the Apostles yet of their own accord added and required voluntary vigils, fastings, and chastity, we may well perceive these works to be wonderful grateful to God, and specially needful in the Clergy. \fq ¹ \ft Voluntary penance.\ef*
\v 6 in chastity, in knowledge, in longanimity, in sweetness, in the Holy Ghost, in charity not feigned,
\v 7 in the word of truth, in the virtue of God; by the armour of justice on the right hand, and on the left,
\v 8 by honour and dishonour, by infamy and good fame: as seducers, and true: as they that are unknown, and known:
\v 9 as dying, and behold we live: as chastened, & not killed:
\v 10 as sorrowful, but always rejoicing: as needy, but enriching many: as \f 1 \fr 6.10 \ft S. Augustine (in Ps. 113) gathereth hereby, that the Apostles did vow poverty.\f* having nothing, and possessing all things.
\v 11 Our mouth is open to you, o Corinthians, our heart is dilated.
\v 12 You are not straitened in us: but in your own bowels you are straitened.
\v 13 But having the same reward (I speak as to my children) be you also dilated.
\v 14 \f 1 \fr 6.14 \ft It is not lawful for Catholics to marry with Heretics or Infidels. See S. Hierom. cont. Iovinian. li. 1. Conc. Laod. c. 10. & 31.\f* Bear not the yoke with infidels. For what participation hath justice with iniquity? or what society is there between light and darkness? \ef - \fr 6.14 \fq What society. \ft ¹ Generally here is forbidden conversation and dealing with all Infidels, and consequently with Heretics, but specially in prayers, or meetings at their Schismatical Service, preaching or other divine office whatsoever. Which the Apostle here uttereth in more particular and different terms, that Christian folk may take the better heed of it. no society (saith he) nor fellowship, no participation nor agreement, no consent between light and darkness, Christ and Baal, the temple of God and the temple of Idols: all Service, as pretended worship of God set up by Heretics or Schismatics, being nothing else but Service of Baal and plain Idolatry, and their conventicles nothing but conspirations against Christ. From such therefore specially we must sever ourselves always in heart and mind, and touching any act of religion in body also, according as the children of Israel were commanded by God to separate themselves ² from the Schismatics Core, Dathan, and Abiron, and their tabernacles, by these words: \+it Depart from the tabernacles of the impious men, and touch ye not those things which pertain to them, lest you be enwrapped in their sins.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Not to communicate with Heretics in any act of religion. \fq ² \ft \+it Num. 16, 26.\+it*\ef*
\v 15 And what agreement with Christ and Belial? or what part hath the faithful with the infidel?
\v 16 And what agreement hath the temple of God with idols? For you are the temple of the living God: as God saith, \x - \xt Lev. 26, 11.\x* \it That I will dwell, and walk in them, and will be their God; and they shall be my people.\it*
\v 17 For the which cause, \x - \xt Esa. 52, 11.\x* \it Go out of the midst of them, and separate yourselves,\it* saith our Lord, \it and touch not the unclean: and I will receive you.\it*
\v 18 \x - \xt Hier. 31, 1.\x* \it and I will be a father to you: and you shall be my sons & daughters, saith our Lord omnipotent.\it*
\c 7
\cd He proceedeth to exhort them to purity, and to receive him into their charity. 3. Which lest they should think he speaketh to accuse them, he commendeth them highly, both for their behaviour toward Titus, and for their penance which they had done upon his other epistle.
\p
\v 1 \sc Having\sc* therefore these promises, my dearest, let us cleanse ourselves from all inquination of the flesh and spirit, perfiting sanctification in the fear of God.
\v 2 Receive us. We have hurt no man, we have corrupted no man, we have circumvented no man.
\v 3 I speak not to your condemnation. For I said before that you are in our hearts to die together and to live together.
\v 4 Much is my confidence with you, much is my glorying for you. I am replenished with consolation; I do exceedingly abound in joy in all our tribulation.
\v 5 For also when we were come into Macedonia, our flesh had no rest, but we suffered all tribulation: without, combats; within, fears.
\v 6 But God that comforteth the humble, did comfort us, in the coming of Titus.
\v 7 And not only in his coming, but also in the consolation, wherewith he was comforted among you, reporting to us your desire, your weeping, your emulation for me, so that I rejoiced the more.
\v 8 For although I made you sorry in an epistle, it repenteth me not: albeit it repented me, seeing that the same epistle (although but for a time) did make you sorry.
\v 9 Now I am glad: not because you were made sorry, but because you were made sorry to penance. For you were made sorry according to God, that in nothing you should suffer detriment by us. \ef - \fr 7.9 \fq Sorry to penance. \ft ¹ The sorrow which a man taketh for worldly losses or any temporal adversity, is not here commended, but that which is and ought to be in all men for their sins past, which is called here, Sorrow towards God and for penance, otherwise called Contrition, and is a thing exceedingly requisite and much praised, the fruits whereof are these that the Apostle reckoneth, working salvation. Which doctrine is far distant from ² Luther's, and Calvin's, and such wicked Libertines, that teach contrition to be all together a means to make sinners either hypocrites, or to put them in despair. \fq ¹ \ft Contrition for a man's sin worketh salvation. \fq ² \ft \+it * To. 2. in assert. art. 6. a Leone damnat.\+it*\ef*
\v 10 For \f 1 \fr 7.10 \ft Contrition or sorrowful lamenting of our offences, is the cause of salvation. Not only faith then saveth, as the Heretics affirm.\f* the sorrow that is according to God, worketh penance unto salvation that is stable: but the sorrow of the world worketh death.
\v 11 For behold this very thing, that you were made sorry according to God, how great carefulness it worketh in you: yea defence, yea indignation, yea fear, yea desire, yea emulation, yea revenge, in all things you have shewed yourselves to be undefiled in the matter.
\v 12 Therefore although I wrote to you, not for him that did the injury, nor for him that suffered: but to manifest our carefulness that we have for you before God,
\v 13 therefore we are comforted. But in our consolation, we did the more aboundantly rejoice upon the joy of Titus, because his spirit was refreshed of all you.
\v 14 And if to him I gloried any thing of you, I am not confounded: but as we spake all things to you in truth, so also our glorying that was to Titus, is made a truth.
\v 15 And his bowels are more aboundantly toward you: remembering the obedience of you all, how with fear and trembling you received him.
\v 16 I rejoice that in all things I have confidence in you.
\c 8
\cd By the example of the poor Macedonians he exhorteth them to contribute largely unto the Church of Hierusalem, 7, & by praising of them, 9. and by the example of Christ, 14. and by their own spiritual profit in being partakers of that Church's merits, 16. and by commending the Collectors that he sendeth.
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* we do you to understand, brethren, the grace of God, that is given in the Churches of Macedonia,
\v 2 that in much experience of tribulation they had aboundance of joy, & their very deep poverty abounded unto the riches of their simplicity,
\v 3 for according to their power (I give them testimony) and above their power they were willing,
\v 4 with much exhortation requesting us the grace and communication of the ministry that is done toward the saints.
\v 5 And not as we hoped, but their own selves they gave, first to our Lord, \f 1 \fr 8.5 \ft The principal respect next after God, is to be had of our masters in religion, in all temporal and spiritual duties.\f* then to us by the will of God:
\v 6 in so much that we desired Titus, that as he began, so also he would perfit in you this grace also.
\v 7 But as in all things you abound in faith, and word, & knowledge, & all carefulness, moreover also in your charity toward us, that in this grace also you may abound.
\v 8 I speak not as commanding: but by the carefulness of others, approving also the good disposition of your charity.
\v 9 \f 1 \fr 8.9 \ft The Epistle for S. Paulinus, \+it Iun. 22.\+it*\f* For you know the grace of our Lord \sc Jesus\sc* Christ, that for you he was made poor, whereas he was rich; that by his poverty you might be rich.
\v 10 And in this point I give counsel: for this is profitable for you, which have begun not only to do, but also to be willing, from the year past:
\v 11 But now perform ye it also in deed: that as your mind is prompt to be willing, so it may be also to perform, of that which you have.
\v 12 For if the will be prompt, it is accepted according to that which it hath, not according to that which it hath not.
\v 13 For not that other should have ease, and you tribulation: but by an equality.
\v 14 Let in this present time your aboundance supply their want: that their aboundance also may supply your want, that there be an equality \ef - \fr 8.14 \fq Aboundance supply. \ft ¹ He meaneth that such as abound in worldly riches, should communicate for supply of other their brethren's necessities, whatsoever they may: that on the other side they whom they help in temporals, may impart to them again some of their spiritual riches, as prayers, and other holy works and graces, which is a happy change and intercourse for the wealthy men, if they could see it. ² And this place proveth plainly that the fastings and satisfactory deeds of one man, be available to others, yea and that holy Saints or other virtuous persons may in measure and proportion of other men's necessities and deservings, allot unto them, as well the supererogation of their spiritual works, as these that abound in worldly goods, may give alms of their superfluities, to them which are in necessity. Which interchange and proportion of things the Apostle doth evidently set down. \fq ¹ \ft Temporal benefits upon spiritual persons. \fq ² \ft One may satisfy and supererogate for another.\ef*
\v 15 as it is written: \x - \xt Exo. 16, 28.\x* \it He that had much, abounded not: and he that had little, wanted not.\it*
\v 16 \f 1 \fr 8.16 \ft The Epistle upon S. Luke's day, \+it Octob. 18.\+it*\f* And thanks be to God, that hath given the self-same carefulness for you in the heart of Titus,
\v 17 for that he admitted indeed exhortation: but being more careful, of his own will he went unto you.
\v 18 We have sent also with him the brother, whose praise is in the Gospel through all the churches:
\v 19 & not only that, but also he was ordained of the churches fellow of our peregrination, for this grace which is ministered of us to the glory of our Lord, and our determined will:
\v 20 avoiding this, lest any man might reprehend us in this fullness that is ministered of us.
\v 21 For we provide good things \x - \xt Ro. 12, 17.\x* not only before God, but also before men.
\v 22 And we have sent with them our brother also, whom we have proved in many things often to be careful: but now much more careful, for the great confidence in you,
\v 23 either for Titus which is my fellow and coadjutor toward you, or our brethren Apostles of the Churches, the glory of Christ.
\v 24 The declaration therefore which is of your charity and our glorying for you, declare ye toward them in the face of the churches.
\c 9
\cd He proceedeth exhorting them to the foresaid contribution, 3. to verify his commending of them. 6. and to do it liberally, that so they may merit the more, and God be the more praised.
\p
\v 1 \sc For\sc* concerning the ministry that is done toward the saints, it is superfluous for me to write unto you. \ef - \fr 9.1 \fq Toward the Saints. \ft ¹ By the Apostle's earnest and often calling upon the Corinthians to give alms for relieving the faithful in distress, the Pastors of God's Church may learn, that it specially pertaineth to their office to be proctors for holy men in prison, poverty, and all other necessity, specially when their want cometh for confession of their faith. \fq ¹ \ft Proctors for Catholic prisoners.\ef*
\v 2 For I know your prompt mind: for the which I glory of you to the Macedonians: That Achaia also is ready from the year past, and your emulation hath provoked very many.
\v 3 But I have sent the brethren, that the thing which we glory of you, be not made void in this behalf, that (as I have said) you may be ready:
\v 4 lest when the Macedonians shall come with me, and find you unready, we (that we say not, ye) may be ashamed \f 1 \fr 9.4 \ft c That is, in this matter of alms Chrysos. Theoph.\f* in this substance.
\v 5 Therefore I thought it necessary to desire the brethren that they would come to you, and prepare this blessing before promised, to be ready so, as a blessing, not as avarice. \ef - \fr 9.5 \fq Not as avarice. \ft ¹ The covetous man that parteth with his penny painfully and with sorrow as though he lost a limb of his body, is noted; and cheerful, ready, voluntary, and large contribution is commended. \fq ¹ \ft Cheerful giving.\ef*
\v 6 \f 1 \fr 9.6 \ft The Epistle for S. Laurence, Aug. 10.\f* And this I say, he that soweth sparingly, sparingly also shall reap: and he that soweth in blessings, of blessings also shall reap. \ef - \fr 9.6 \fq Soweth sparingly. \ft ¹ Alms is compared to seed. For as the seed thrown into the ground, though it seem to be cast away, yet is not lost, but is laid up in certain hope of great increase: so that which men give in alms, though it seem to be cast away and to perish in respect of the giver, yet indeed it is most fruitful, the benefit thereof manifoldly returning to him again. Whereupon the Apostle's conclusion is clear, that according to the measure of the alms or seeding (which is more or less in respect of the will and ability of the giver) the increase and aboundance of harvest, that is, of grace and glory shall ensue. See S. Augustine \+it in Psal. 49. circa med. & q. 4. ad Dulcitium.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft The greater alms, the greater merit and reward.\ef*
\v 7 Every one as he hath determined in his heart, not of sadness or of necessity. For \x - \xt Ecclci 35, 11.\x* \it God loveth a cheerful giver.\it*
\v 8 And God is able to make all grace abound in you: that in all things always having all sufficiency, you may abound unto all good works,
\v 9 as it is written: \x - \xt Ps. 111, 9.\x* \it He distributed, he gave to the poor: \it* \f 2 \fr 9.9 \ft The fruit of alms is the increase of grace in all justice and good works to life everlasting: God giving these things for reward & recompense of charitable works, which therefore be called the seed or meritorious cause of these spiritual fruits.\f* \it his justice\it* remaineth for ever.
\v 10 And he that ministereth seed to the sower, will give bread also for to eat: and will multiply your seed and will augment the increases of the fruits of your justice:
\v 11 that being enriched in all things, you may abound unto all simplicity, which worketh by us thanks-giving to God.
\v 12 Because the ministry of this office doth not only supply those things that the Saints want, but aboundeth also by many thanks-givings in our Lord, \ef - \fr 9.12 \fq Doth not only supply. \ft ¹ When alms are given, specially to holy men, not only the givers obtain great benefits thereby, and the wants of others be supplied, but God also by the receiver's continual prayers and thanks-giving therefore, is exceedingly honoured: so that charity bestowed in this sort, is an act of God's worship and of religion. \fq ¹ \ft Alms redound to God's honour.\ef*
\v 13 by the proof of this ministry, glorifying God in the obedience of your confession unto the Gospel of Christ, and in the simplicity of communicating unto them, and unto all,
\v 14 and in their praying for you, being desirous of you because of the excellent grace of God in you.
\v 15 Thanks be to God for his unspeakable gift.
\c 10
\cd Against the false Apostles granting the infirmity of his person, he doth notwithstanding set out the power of his Apostleship, 12. reprehending them also for challenging to themselves the praise of other men's labours.
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* I Paul myself beseech you by the mildness and modesty of Christ, who in presence indeed am humble among you, but absent am bold on you.
\v 2 But I beseech you, that being present I need not be bold by that confidence wherewith I am thought to be bold against some: which think us as though we walk according to the flesh.
\v 3 For walking in the flesh, we war not according to the flesh.
\v 4 For the weapons of our warfare are not carnal: but mighty to God unto the destruction of munitions, destroying counsels, \ef - \fr 10.4 \fq Weapons. \ft ¹ He meaneth the ample spiritual and Apostolical power given by Christ for the punishment of false Apostles, Heretics, and rebels to God's Church, who are here noted specially by pride and insolence (which is the proper mark of such fellows) ² to extol themselves above the measure of the science of God, which consisteth in humble obedience to the faith and the preachers of the same. \fq ¹ \ft Punishing of Heretics. \fq ² \ft Their pride.\ef*
\v 5 and all loftiness extolling itself against the knowledge of God, and bringing into captivity all understanding unto the obedience of Christ,
\v 6 and having in a readiness to revenge all disobedience, when your obedience shall be fulfilled. \ef - \fr 10.6 \fq To revenge. \ft ¹ You may see hereby, that the spiritual power of Bishops is not only in preaching the Gospel, and so by persuasion and exhortation only (as some Heretics hold) to remit or retain sins, but that it hath authority to punish, judge, and condemn Heretics and other like rebels: which power ² one of the principal rebels of this time being convinced by the evidence of the place, acknowledgeth to be grounded upon Christ's word, \+it Whatsoever you bind in earth, shall be bound in heaven: Mat. 18, 18.\+it* applying also the words spoken to Hieremy (c. 1, 10.) \+it Behold I appoint thee over Nations and kingdoms, that thou plant, pluck up, build and destroy\+it*, to confirm and explicate the power Apostolic here alleged by S. Paul. Mary they would gladly draw this power from the lawful successors of the Apostles, to themselves, their ministers, and consistories ³. Which are nothing else but the shops and Councels of sedition and all the conspiracies of this time, against the lawful Princes of the world. \fq ¹ \ft The spiritual power of Bishops against Heretics. \fq ² \ft \+it Calvin upon this place.\+it* \fq ³ \ft Heretical Consistories.\ef*
\v 7 See the things that are according to appearance. If any man have affiance in himself, that he is Christ's: let him think this again with himself, that as he is Christ's, so we also.
\v 8 For and if I should glory some-what more of our power, which our Lord hath given us unto edification and not to your destruction; I shall not be ashamed. \ef - \fr 10.8 \fq Unto edification. \ft ¹ This great power of the Church's censures, specially of Excommunication, as it was given for the good and salvation of the people, so it must not be used against the innocent: no nor yet upon Heretics or other offenders, but where and when it may be likelihood benefit either the parties, or the people, or may be executed without the hurt or perturbation of the whole Church, as often times it cannot be, by reason of the multitude of offenders. Which caused the Apostle here to signify that he would not use his uttermost authority against the false Apostles which disturbed them, till themselves were in perfect obedience unto him, lest by punishing the principal offenders, a greater disturbance and revolt might fall among the people, if they were not before in perfect obedience. \fq ¹ \ft Ecclesiastical censures (namely Excommunication) when & where to be executed.\ef*
\v 9 But that I may not be thought as it were to terrify you by epistles
\v 10 (for his epistles indeed, say they, are sore and vehement; but his bodily presence weak, & his speech contemptible)
\v 11 let him this think that is such a one, that such as we are in word by epistles, absent: such also we are in deed, present.
\v 12 For we dare not match or compare ourselves with certain, that commend themselves: but we measure ourselves in ourselves, & compare ourselves to ourselves.
\v 13 But we will not glory above our measure: but according to the measure of the rule; which God hath measured to us, a measure to reach even unto you.
\v 14 For not, as though we reached not unto you, do we extend ourselves beyond. For we are come as far as to you in the Gospel of Christ.
\v 15 not glorying above measure in other men's labours: but having \f 1 \fr 10.15 \ft \+it c ἐλπίδα ἀυξομένης τῆς πίςεως ὑμῶν.\+it*\f* hope of your faith increasing, to be magnified in you according to our rule aboundantly,
\v 16 yea unto those places that are beyond you, to evangelize, not in another man's rule, to glory in those things that are prepared before.
\v 17 \x - \xt Ier. 9, 23.\x* But he that glorieth, let him glory in our Lord. \f 2 \fr 10.17 \ft The Epistle upon S. Lucy's day 13. Decem. S. Martha's. Iulij 29. S. Agnes. secundo Ian. 28. S. Potentiana's. May 19.\f*
\v 18 For not he that commendeth himself, the same is approved; but whom God commendeth.
\c 11
\cd He reasoneth the matter with the Corinthians, why they should prefer the false Apostles before him. And because they give them leave to brag and commend themselves and to abuse them so miserably, he trusteth they will also give him the hearing: 21. and so he beginneth, and first shewing himself in all Judaical respects (wherein only stood all their boasting) to be as they are, he addeth afterward such a long roll of his suffering for Christ, as is imcomparable.
\p
\v 1 Would God you could bear some little of my folly: but do ye also support me:
\v 2 for I emulate you with the emulation of God. For I have \f 1 \fr 11.2 \ft The Apostles and their successors did despouse the people whom they converted, to Christ, in all purity & Charity of truth, and wholly undefiled and void of error and heresy.\f* despoused you to one man, to present you a chaste virgin unto Christ.
\v 3 \x - \xt Gen. 3, 4.\x* But I fear lest, as the serpent seduced Eve by his subtlety, so your senses may be corrupted, & fall from the simplicity that is in Christ. \ef - \fr 11.3 \fq From the simplicity. \ft ¹ People fall from their first faith, virginity, and simplicity in Christ, not by sodden revolt, but by little & little, in giving ear to the subtile persuasion of the Serpent, speaking to them by the sweet mouths & allurements of Heretics. Of which kind of seduction he giveth Eve for an example, who was by her greedy desire of knowledge and the Devils promise of the same, drawn from the native simplicity and obedience to God. As at this day, promise and pretence of knowledge driveth many a poor soul from the sure, true, sincere, and only belief of God's Church. \fq ¹ \ft As Eve by the Serpent, so the people are seduced by Heretics.\ef*
\v 4 For if he that \f 1 \fr 11.4 \ft The note of a false teacher, \+it to come:\+it* that is, without lawful calling or sending to thrust and intrude himself into another man's charge.\f* cometh, preach another Christ whom we have not preached, or you receive another spirit whom you have not received; or another Gospel which you have not received: you might well suffer it.
\v 5 For I suppose that I have done nothing less than the great Apostles.
\v 6 For although rude in speech, yet not in knowledge. But in all things we are made manifest to you. \ef - \fr 11.6 \fq Rude in speech. \ft ¹ Hereby we see that the seditious and false teachers have often the gift of eloquence whereby the simple be easily beguiled. Such were Core and Dathan, as Josephus writeth \+it Antiq. li. 4 c. 2.\+it* for the same, S. Augustine \+it (li. 5 Confes. c. 3. & 13.)\+it* calleth the Heretic Faustus Manichæus, \+it magnum laqueum Diaboli, a great snare of the Devil\+it*, saying that he passed the glorious Doctor S. Ambrose in shew of words, but far inferior to him (without all comparison) in substance and matter ². In which sort the Apostle here is glad to compare himself with the false Apostles, whom the Corinthians did follow and extol far above him by reason of their eloquence; granting to them that gift, but challenging to himself superiority in knowledge, which all wise men prefer before vain words. ³ And it is the bane of our poor country, that the people now a days give credit rather to new orators and foolish younkers, for their sweet speeches; than to the glorious Doctors of Christ's Church, for their singular knowledge and more grave eloquence. \fq ¹ \ft Heretics sometime eloquent. \fq ² \ft Knowledge better than gay words. \fq ³ \ft Young orators among heretics preferred before the ancient Doctors.\ef*
\v 7 Or did I commit a sin, humbling myself, that you might be exalted? because I evangelized unto you the Gospel of God gratis?
\v 8 Other churches I spoiled, taking a stipend, for your ministry.
\v 9 And when I was with you, and had need, I was burdenous to none: for that which I wanted, the brethren supplied that came from Macedonia: and in all things I have kept myself without burden to you, and will keep.
\v 10 The truth of Christ is in me, that this glorying shall not be infringed toward me in the countries of Achaia.
\v 11 Wherefore? because I love you not? God doth know.
\v 12 But that which I do, I will also do, that I may cut away the occasion of them that desire occasion: that, in that which they glory, they may be found even like us.
\v 13 For such false apostles are \f 1 \fr 11.13 \ft A proper term for Heretics that shape themselves into the habit of true teachers, specially by often allegation and commendation of the Scriptures. Read the notable admonition of the ancient writer \+it Vincentius Lirinensis\+it* in his golden book \+it Against the Profane novelties of all heresies.\+it*\f* crafty workers, transfiguring themselves into Apostles of Christ.
\v 14 And no marvel: for Satan himself transfigureth himself into an Angel of light.
\v 15 It is no great matter therefore if his ministers be transfigured as the ministers of justice: whose end shall be according to their works.
\v 16 Again I say, (let no man think me to be foolish: otherwise take me as foolish, that I also may glory a little,)
\v 17 that which I speak, I speak not according to God, but as it were in foolishness, in this substance of glorying.
\v 18 Because many glory according to the flesh, I also will glory.
\v 19 \f 1 \fr 11.19 \ft The Epistle upon the Sunday of Sexagesima.\f* For you do gladly suffer the foolish: whereas yourselves are wise.
\v 20 For you suffer if a man bring you into servitude, if a man devour, if a man take, if a man be extolled, if a man strike you on the face.
\v 21 I speak according to dishonour, as though we had been weak in this part. Wherein any man dare (I speak foolishly) I dare also.
\v 22 \x - \xt Phil. 3, 5.\x* They are Hebrews: and I. They are Israelites: and I. They are the seed of Abraham; and I.
\v 23 They are the ministers of Christ; and I. (I speak as one scarce wise) more I: in many more labours, in prisons more aboundantly, in stripes above measure, in deaths often.
\v 24 Of the Jews five times, did I receive \x - \xt Deu. 25, 3.\x* forty, saving one.
\v 25 Thrice was I beaten \x - \xt Act. 16, 23.\x* with rods, \x - \xt 14, 18.\x* once I was stoned, thrice I suffered \x - \xt 27, 15.\x* shipwreck; night and day have I been in the depth of the sea,
\v 26 in journeying often, perils of waters, perils of thieves, perils of my Nation, perils of Gentiles, perils in the city, perils in the wilderness, perils in the sea, perils among false brethren,
\v 27 in labour and misery, in much watchings, in hunger and thirst, in fastings often, in cold and nakedness,
\v 28 beside those things which are outwardly: my daily \f 1 \fr 11.28 \ft \+it b ἐπισύςασις.\+it* S. Chrysostom and Theophylacte interpret it of daily conspiracy against him: others, of multitude of cares instant & urgent upon him.\f* instance, the carefulness of all churches.
\v 29 Who is weak, and I \f 1 \fr 11.29 \ft \+it c non uror? πυροῦμαι ?\+it*\f* am not weak? Who is scandalized, and I am not burnt?
\v 30 If I must glory: I will glory of the things that concern my infirmity.
\v 31 The God and Father of our Lord \sc Jesus\sc* Christ, who is blessed for ever, knoweth that I lie not.
\v 32 \x - \xt Act. 9, 24.\x* At Damascus the Governor of the nation under Aretas the king, kept the city of the Damascenes for to apprehend me:
\v 33 and through a window in a basket was I let down by the wall, and so escaped his hands.
\c 12
\cd He telleth of his incomparable visions, 5. but for humility liketh better to talk of his infirmities: 11. putting the fault in the Corinthians for that he is fain thus to rehearse his own commendations. 13. Where again he reasoneth the matter with them like a father, why they should prefer those false Apostles before him. 20. And feareth lest at his coming he shall be compelled to excommunicate many of them.
\p
\v 1 IF I must glory (it is not expedient indeed) but I will come to the visions & revelations of our Lord. \ef - \fr 12.1 \fq Visions. \ft ¹ S. Cyprian \+it (ep. 69. nu. 4.)\+it* complaineth that the Adversaries of God's Church and Priests, give no credit to visions. But their incredulity is much more in our days, that condemn all such revelations, though they be reported and recorded for most certain, of holy S. Gregory, S. Bede, or who-else soever. Yea they are so wicked in this case, that the vision which the holy author of the book of Machabees ² calleth \+it fide dignum\+it*, worthy of credit, is one cause why they deny the whole book to be Canonical: and as well might they for this vision deny all S. Paul's Epistles, and for the like, the Acts of the Apostles, \+it Act. 9. 10. 11. 12. 27\+it*: and the Gospel itself, \+it Mat. 1, 20. 2. 13. 19.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Visions have no credit with heretics. \fq ² \ft \+it 2. Macha. 15, 11.\+it*\ef*
\v 2 I know a man in Christ above fourteen years ago (whether in the body, I know not, or out of the body, I know not: God doth know) such a one \f 1 \fr 12.2 \ft By this we may prove that it is neither impossible, incredible, nor undecent, that is reported by the ancient Fathers of some that have been ravished or rapt (whether in body or out of body god knoweth) & brought to see the state of the next life, as well of the saved as damned.\f* rapt even to the third Heaven.
\v 3 And I know such a man (whether in the body, or out of the body, I know not; God doth know)
\v 4 that he was rapt into Paradise; & heard secret words, which it is not lawful for a man to speak.
\v 5 For such an one I will glory: but for myself I will glory nothing, saving in my infirmities.
\v 6 For and if I will glory, I shall not be foolish: for I shall say truth. But I spare, lest any man should esteem me above that which he seeth in me, or heareth any thing of me.
\v 7 And lest the greatness of the revelations might extol me, there was given me a prick of my flesh, an angel of Satan, to buffet me.
\v 8 For the which thing thrice I besought our Lord, that it might depart from me.
\v 9 And he said to me: My grace sufficeth thee, for power is perfited in infirmity. Gladly therefore will I glory in mine infirmity, that the power of Christ may dwell in me.
\v 10 For the which cause I please myself in infirmities, in contumelies, in necessities, in persecutions, in distresses for Christ. For when I am weak, then am I mighty.
\v 11 I am become foolish: you have compelled me. For I ought to have been commended of you: for I have been nothing less than they that are above measure Apostles: although I am nothing. \ef - \fr 12.11 \fq Above measure Apostles. \ft ¹ Though all were in that they were Apostles, of one and the same order, yet we may see that some had marvelous great preeminence and privilege above others in the same office: specially S. Peter and S. John, whom S. Paul often calleth \+it great Apostles, above measure or passing Apostles, the pillars, &c.\+it* 2 Cor. 11, 5. 12, 11. Gal. 2, 9. \fq ¹ \ft The Apostles some greater than others.\ef*
\v 12 Yet the signs of my Apostleship have been done upon you in all patience, in signs & wonders & mighty deeds. \ef - \fr 12.12 \fq In signs. \ft ¹ Miracles be necessary, and be great signs of truth, when it is first newly taught. And therefore let all Catholic men hold fast that faith which was first preached and confirmed by miracles. As in England by S. Augustine, and in other nations by other holy Apostolic men. And let the Heretics that preach extraordinarily, newly, and otherwise than we received at our first conversion, shew their calling and doctrine by miracles, or else let them be taken for false Apostles as they be. \fq ¹ \ft We must stick to the faith first planted by miracles.\ef*
\v 13 For what is there that you have had less than the other churches; but that I myself have not burdened you? Pardon me this injury.
\v 14 Behold, now the third time I am ready to come to you; & I will not be burdenous unto you. For I seek not the things that are yours, but you. For neither ought the children lay up treasures for the parents, but the parents for the children.
\v 15 But I most gladly will bestow, & will myself moreover be bestowed for your souls: although loving you more, I am loved less.
\v 16 But be it so: I have not burdened you: but being crafty, I took you by guile.
\v 17 Have I circumvented you by any of them whom I sent to you?
\v 18 I requested Titus, and I sent with him a brother. Did Titus circumvent you? walked we not with one spirit? not in the self-same steps?
\v 19 \f 1 \fr 12.19 \fq Of old \ft \+it Again\+it*\f* Of old think you that we excuse ourselves to you? Before God, in Christ we speak: but all things (my dearest) for your edifying.
\v 20 For I fear lest perhaps when I come, I find you not such as I would: and I be found of you, such an one as you would not. Lest perhaps contentions, emulations, stomachings, dissensions, detractions, whisperings, swellings, seditions be among you.
\v 21 Lest again when I come, God humble me among you: & I mourn many of them that sinned before, & \f 1 \fr 12.21 \ft \+it τῶν μὴ μετανοησάντων.\+it* Which S. Augustine saith \+it (ep. 108.)\+it* is spoken here of doing great penance for heinous sins, as \+it poenitentes\+it* did in the primitive Church. So that it is not only to repent or to amend their lives, as the Protestants translate it.\f* have not done penance for the uncleanness & fornication and incontinency that they have committed.
\c 13
\cd He driveth into them the fear of excommunication: to the end that they doing penance before-hand, he may not be compelled to use his authority when he cometh, and as he hath threatened. 11. And so with a general exhortation he endeth.
\p
\v 1 LO this the third time I come unto you: \x - \xt Deu. 19, 15.\x* In the mouth of two or three witnesses shall every word stand.
\v 2 I foretold and do foretell as present, and now absent, to them that sinned before, and all the rest, that if I come again, I will not spare.
\v 3 Seek you an experiment of him that speaketh in me, Christ; who in you is not weak, but is mighty in you?
\v 4 For although he was crucified of infirmity; yet he liveth by the power of God. For we also are weak in him: but we shall live with him by the power of God on you.
\v 5 Try your own selves if you be in the faith, prove ye yourselves. Know you not yourselves that Christ \sc Jesus\sc* is in you, unless perhaps you be reprobates. \ef - \fr 13.5 \fq Try yourselves. \ft ¹ The Heretics argue hereupon, that every man may know himself certainly to be in grace: where the Apostle speaketh expressly and only of faith. The act whereof a man may know and feel to be in himself, because it is an act of understanding, though he cannot be assured that he hath his sins remitted, and that he is in all points in state of grace and salvation: because every man that is of the Catholic faith, is not always of good life agreeable thereunto, nor the acts of our will so subject to understanding, that we can know certainly whether we be good or evil. See. S. Augustine \+it to. 7. de perfect. iustitiæ c. 15. Li. de Cor. et grat. c. 13. &\+it* S. Thomas \+it 1. 2. q. 112. art. 5.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft We may know that we have faith, but not that we are in grace.\ef*
\v 6 But I hope you \f 1 \fr 13.6 \fq know \ft shall know\f* know that we are not reprobates.
\v 7 And we pray God, that you do no evil, not that we may appear approved, but that you may do that which is good, and we be as reprobates.
\v 8 For we cannot any thing against the truth; but for the truth.
\v 9 For we rejoice, for that we are weak, and you are mighty. This also we pray for, your consummation.
\v 10 Therefore these things I write absent: that being present I may not deal hardly according to the \f 1 \fr 13.10 \ft c Ecclesiastical power to punish offenders by the censures of the Church.\f* power which our Lord hath given me unto edification and not unto destruction.
\v 11 \x - \xt c παρακαλεῖσθε, Ro. 16, 16. 1. Cor. 16, 20.\x* For the rest, brethren, rejoice, be perfect, \f 2 \fr 13.11 \ft The Epistle in a votive Mass of the B. Trinity.\f* take exhortation, be of one mind, have peace; and the God of peace & love shall be with you.
\v 12 Salute one another in a holy kiss. All the saints salute you.
\v 13 The grace of our Lord \sc Jesus\sc* Christ, and the charity of God, and the communication of the Holy Ghost be with you all. Amen.
